# Line endings, decided here rather than by each contributor's Git install.
#
# Git for Windows' default installer option sets core.autocrlf=true, which
# rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. Everything here is LF, so on a Windows
# clone that conversion lands CRLF in files that are then executed or parsed
# inside a Linux container -- where a trailing \r is not whitespace, it is
# part of the token:
#
#   scripts/seed-demo-content.sh  docker-compose.local.yml mounts it and runs
#                                 `sh /seed.sh`. With CRLF it dies on the
#                                 first line and the portal comes up empty.
#                                 Nothing depends on the seed service, so the
#                                 stack still starts and the failure is quiet
#                                 -- an empty portal, no obvious cause.
#   scripts/run-local.sh          the other one-command path onto a laptop.
#   nginx.conf.template           rendered by envsubst into nginx's config, so
#                                 a trailing \r ends up inside header VALUES.
#
# java-backend/mvnw was already safe: java-backend/.gitattributes (generated
# by the Maven wrapper) pins it to LF for that subtree. This file gives the
# rest of the repository the same protection instead of leaving it to one
# tool's generated config to have covered the right files by luck.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the repository AND checks out LF on
# every platform, so none of this depends on how a contributor installed Git.
# Windows tooling has handled LF for years; the exceptions are below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe genuinely needs CRLF: a .bat/.cmd with LF-only endings can split
# tokens across lines mid-command. These run on the host, never in a container.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touched. Marked explicitly so `text=auto` cannot guess wrong and
# corrupt one by "normalising" bytes that happen to look like text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jar  binary
*.xlsx binary
*.db   binary
*.sqlite3 binary
